WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.820
A ball of cells has to become a body with an outside, a middle and a gut.

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.820
How do surface cells get inside, and does it matter whom they meet?

3
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:18.570
The first film showed cleavage cutting the egg into cells, each with its own slice.

4
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.770
Purple marked what a cell inherits.

5
00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:27.610
Cleavage makes new contacts as cells divide, but it does not rearrange the whole

6
00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.610
embryo.

7
00:00:29.183 --> 00:00:31.543
Gastrulation is the stage that does.

8
00:00:33.103 --> 00:00:37.303
Cells travel from the surface to the inside, and sort into three germ layers.

9
00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:40.683
Ectoderm, in blue, stays outside.

10
00:00:42.203 --> 00:00:44.103
Mesoderm, in red, forms the middle.

11
00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:47.683
Endoderm, in yellow, lines the inside.

12
00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:51.777
Embryos get cells inside by several movements.

13
00:00:53.077 --> 00:00:57.957
In invagination, a sheet bends inward, like a finger pushed into a soft ball.

14
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:04.127
In involution, a sheet rolls over an edge and spreads along the inside.

15
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:09.967
In a frog, cells roll in over the lip of the blastopore, the opening where they

16
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:10.967
enter.

17
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:16.367
In ingression, single cells leave the sheet and move inward, one at a time.

18
00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:20.487
Embryos combine these movements to bring cells inside.

19
00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:25.657
Moving inside does something else.

20
00:01:27.057 --> 00:01:29.737
Cells now sit next to cells they never touched before.

21
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:33.017
Can a neighbour change what a cell becomes?

22
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.890
Hilde Mangold tested this with newt embryos.

23
00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:42.710
She cut out a piece of the dorsal lip of the blastopore, the edge on the future back

24
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:43.710
side.

25
00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:46.393
The donor came from a pale newt species.

26
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:52.013
She grafted the piece onto the belly side of a darker embryo, from a related

27
00:01:52.013 --> 00:01:55.813
species. The host grew a second body axis.

28
00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:04.287
If the graft built that second axis only from its own inherited material, what

29
00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:05.727
colour would the new axis be?

30
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.520
It was not all pale.

31
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.580
Dark host cells were part of it.

32
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:18.920
The graft had recruited them.

33
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.840
It told host cells what to become.

34
00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:26.723
Remember the crescent: colour showed where it went, not what it did.

35
00:02:27.923 --> 00:02:29.723
Here colour still only traced cells.

36
00:02:30.903 --> 00:02:34.523
The graft was the test, and colour showed host cells joined in.

37
00:02:35.517 --> 00:02:40.557
A signal from one group of cells that changes the fate of its neighbours is called

38
00:02:40.557 --> 00:02:44.617
induction. The grafted tissue is called the organizer.

39
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:47.770
So a cell's fate has two sources.

40
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.450
Purple: what it inherits from its slice of egg.

41
00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.750
Green: what its new neighbours tell it.

42
00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:57.890
Most embryos studied closely use both.
